      "day": 1,
      "scheduled_at": "2026-10-20T09:00:00+05:30",
      "topic": "Yield Curve Inversion and Recession Signals",
      "status": "Done",
      "attempts": 0,
      "job_id": null,
      "claimed_at": null,
      "youtube_video_id": null,
      "youtube_url": null,
      "published_at": null,
      "last_error": null,
      "script_full": "Did you know an inverted yield curve has preceded nearly every US recession, yet it predicts timing poorly? When short-term Treasury yields exceed long-term yields, the curve flips downward, signaling tighter policy and slower growth expectations. Since 1969, every US recession has followed an inversion, according to Federal Reserve history, but false signals can linger for months. In India watch the RBI repo rate, government bond yields, Nifty b",
      "note": "imported from Excel"
    },
    "D01-15:00": {
      "row_index": 3,
      "day": 1,
      "scheduled_at": "2026-10-20T15:00:00+05:30",
      "topic": "Free Cash Flow vs Net Profit",
      "status": "Done",
      "attempts": 0,
      "job_id": null,
      "claimed_at": null,
      "youtube_video_id": null,
      "youtube_url": null,
      "published_at": null,
      "last_error": null,
      "script_full": "Most investors don't realize that a company can report massive profits while its bank account slowly bleeds dry every single day. Net profit includes non-cash accounting items like depreciation. Free cash flow shows actual money left after paying all necessary capital expenditures. Look at many high growth tech firms. They show brilliant accounting earnings yet burn billions in negative free cash flow annually. On the NSE, Indian manufacturing gi",
      "note": "imported from Excel"
    },
    "D01-20:00": {
      "row_index": 4,
      "day": 1,
      "scheduled_at": "2026-10-20T20:00:00+05:30",
      "topic": "Discounted Cash Flow (DCF) Basics",
      "status": "Done",
      "attempts": 0,
      "job_id": null,
      "claimed_at": null,
      "youtube_video_id": null,
      "youtube_url": null,
      "published_at": null,
      "last_error": null,
      "script_full": "Most investors don't realize that a company is actually worth just the present value of its future cash flows. Money tomorrow is worth less than money today. We use a discount rate to shrink future earnings down into today's actual value. If you apply a higher discount rate, perceived risk shoots up, drastically reducing the total calculated valuation of the entire business. When RBI interest rates shift across India, the discount rate changes, d",
      "note": "imported from Excel"
    },
    "D02-09:00": {
      "row_index": 5,
      "day": 2,
      "scheduled_at": "2026-10-21T09:00:00+05:30",
      "topic": "P/E Ratio and Earnings Yield",
      "status": "Done",
      "attempts": 0,
      "job_id": null,
      "claimed_at": null,
      "youtube_video_id": null,
      "youtube_url": null,
      "published_at": null,
      "last_error": null,
      "script_full": "Most investors don't realize that the classic price-to-earnings ratio is actually just the inverse of your earnings yield. If a company trades at a P/E of twenty, its earnings yield is five percent, calculated by dividing one by twenty. You must compare this stock earnings yield directly against risk-free bond yields to see if equities are genuinely attractive. In the Indian markets, when NSE stock P/E ratios soar high, earnings yields drop below",
      "note": "imported from Excel"
    },
    "D02-15:00": {
      "row_index": 6,
      "day": 2,
      "scheduled_at": "2026-10-21T15:00:00+05:30",
      "topic": "EV/EBITDA vs P/E",
      "status": "Done",
      "attempts": 0,
      "job_id": "D02-15:00_1792600640",
      "claimed_at": "2026-10-21T22:07:19.372770+05:30",
      "youtube_video_id": "VdxFhvJLxFE",
      "youtube_url": "https://youtube.com/shorts/VdxFhvJLxFE",
      "published_at": "2026-10-21T22:10:17.165987+05:30",
      "last_error": null,
      "script_full": "Why do smart investors ignore the standard PE ratio and use EV to EBITDA instead for better stock valuation? The PE ratio only looks at share price and earnings, while enterprise value includes total debt and cash in hand. If a company has massive debt, its PE ratio looks artificially cheap until high interest payments crush their actual operating profits. On the NSE, capital-heavy manufacturing firms in India often mask high borrowing costs until EV to EBITDA exposes the truth. Always use EV to EBITDA when comparing heavily indebted companies to find true value without nasty debt surprises. Follow the journey to improve your financial knowledge. Drop your questions below."
    },
    "D02-20:00": {
      "row_index": 7,
      "day": 2,
      "scheduled_at": "2026-10-21T20:00:00+05:30",
      "topic": "Bond Duration and Interest Rate Risk",
      "status": "Done",
      "attempts": 0,
      "job_id": "D02-20:00_1792612291",
      "claimed_at": "2026-10-22T01:21:31.516969+05:30",
      "youtube_video_id": "-uqMKTS48sU",
      "youtube_url": "https://youtube.com/shorts/-uqMKTS48sU",
      "published_at": "2026-10-22T01:23:33.840848+05:30",
      "last_error": null,
      "script_full": "Did you know rising interest rates can secretly crush your safe bond portfolio without any warning whatsoever? Bond duration measures price sensitivity. Higher duration means your bond prices drop significantly harder when market interest rates climb higher. If a ten-year government bond has seven years duration, a one percent rate hike destroys seven percent value. In India, RBI repo rate hikes directly hammer long-duration gilt mutual funds, wiping out months of fixed interest gains. Always match your investment horizon with bond duration to protect your hard-earned capital from nasty market volatility. Follow the journey to improve your financial knowledge. Drop your questions below."
    },
    "D03-09:00": {
      "row_index": 8,
      "day": 3,
      "scheduled_at": "2026-10-22T09:00:00+05:30",
      "topic": "Operating vs Financial Leverage",
      "status": "Done",
      "attempts": 0,
      "job_id": "D03-09:00_1792659137",
      "claimed_at": "2026-10-22T14:22:16.936538+05:30",
      "youtube_video_id": "dl-5WOPx1OI",
      "youtube_url": "https://youtube.com/shorts/dl-5WOPx1OI",
      "published_at": "2026-10-22T14:24:13.091852+05:30",
      "last_error": null,
      "script_full": "Did you know leverage can explode your profits or completely destroy your business overnight? Let us decode this financial puzzle. Operating leverage relies on massive fixed costs like machinery. Financial leverage depends entirely on debt and interest payments. Companies with high operating leverage see huge profit jumps when sales rise, but massive losses when demand drops. In India, NSE manufacturing firms manage high operating leverage, while real estate developers juggle heavy debt interest. Smart investors always check a company's debt ratio before buying shares to avoid sudden bankruptcy risks. Follow the journey to improve your financial knowledge. Drop your questions below."
    },
    "D03-15:00": {
      "row_index": 9,
      "day": 3,
      "scheduled_at": "2026-10-22T15:00:00+05:30",
      "topic": "Margin Trading and Margin Calls",
      "status": "Done",
      "attempts": 0,
      "job_id": "D03-15:00_1792674112",
      "claimed_at": "2026-10-22T18:31:52.225367+05:30",
      "youtube_video_id": "WdYUtZgAGro",
      "youtube_url": "https://youtube.com/shorts/WdYUtZgAGro",
      "published_at": "2026-10-22T18:39:56.304499+05:30",
      "last_error": null,
      "script_full": "Did you know why Margin Trading and Margin Calls is watched closely by the world's best investors?  When this happens, smart money repositions immediately. Historically, every major shift in this metric has triggered major moves in equity markets. In the Indian market, this directly impacts Nifty volatility and corporate debt costs. The golden rule: Never fight the macro trend. Protect your capital first. Follow for daily finance intelligence. Share this with an investor friend."
    },
    "D03-20:00": {
      "row_index": 10,
      "day": 3,
      "scheduled_at": "2026-10-22T20:00:00+05:30",
      "topic": "Call and Put Options Payoff",
      "status": "Done",
      "attempts": 0,
      "job_id": "D03-20:00_1792679801",
      "claimed_at": "2026-10-22T20:06:41.237311+05:30",
      "youtube_video_id": "30MEE6IONdk",
      "youtube_url": "https://youtube.com/shorts/30MEE6IONdk",
      "published_at": "2026-10-22T20:09:42.829402+05:30",
      "last_error": null,
      "script_full": "Are you losing money on options because you do not understand payoff graphs? Watch this quick breakdown. A call option payoff rises infinitely as stock prices climb, whereas a put option profits when the market drops hard. Historical Nifty data shows that buying naked options without checking payoff limits destroys over eighty percent of retail trader capital. On the National Stock Exchange, Indian traders use strike prices in rupees to calculate exact breakeven points before entering trades. Always map your maximum risk and reward on a payoff chart before risking your hard earned money in derivatives. Follow the journey to improve your financial knowledge. Drop your questions below."
    },
